    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5475165/admissions-associate": {
      "title": "Admissions Associate",
      "description": "Admissions Associate Salary $43,888.00 Annually Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Full-Time Job Number POS-761 Division Advancement and Enrollment Management Department AEM-Admissions Opening Date 09/08/2026 Closing Date Continuous Description Benefits Job Description Summary JOB PURPOSE STATEMENT The Admissions Associate is responsible for the overall management of student recruiting for an assigned geographic territory from prospect and inquiry to applied, admitted, and enrolled stages. This person will provide leadership and oversight of all recruitment and enrollment activities and initiatives within their given territory, including initiation and maintenance of referral sources and K-12 school relationships; management of an individual student prospect portfolio; and coordination of on-campus and field recruiting events. This position is expected to meet stated performance standards and benchmarks, as well as perform at or above goal in all areas. Review of applications will begin Friday, September 25, 2026. Principal Duties and Responsibilities FUNCTIONS: Recruitment, admissions and enrollment Onboarding coordination Performance reporting DUTIES AND RESPONIBILITIES : 1. Recruitment, admissions and enrollment Plan, develop, implement and monitor admissions recruitment programs while maintaining workloads, work-flows, deadlines, work objectives and time utilization of assigned programs. Provides individual and group presentations which accurately convey information about SSU and its programs to prospective students, parents, counselors and others to include application, admission and selection processes, campus features, academic major and required prerequisites, undergraduate college and graduation requirements, academic and support services, financial aid and scholarship information, campus life and other information as appropriate. Organize, create and participate in off-campus and on-campus recruiting events including, but not limited to preview days, college fairs, classroom presentations, sessions, community outreach events, and high school and community college events. Disseminate information about SSU over the telephone and during on-campus visits to prospective students and parents. Responsible for answering phone calls, and answering large volumes of electronic and hardcopy correspondence received by the Office of Admissions in a timely manner. The staff member will also make outbound calls and host information sessions to prospective students. Researches, interprets, clarifies, and adjudicates a variety of problems dealing with, but not limited to, admissions, registration, matriculation, residency, student academic status, course class conflicts, and fees. Works to complete all incomplete applications, as well as solicit applications from non-applied inquiries and solicit deposits from admitted students. Assist in the research and development of contact lists for potential new marketing and recruitment channels, partnerships, and outreach. Cultivate partnerships and pipeline relationships with other organizations and institutions, such as school districts and professional organizations. 2. Onboarding Coordination Assist students with the various steps required in the preparation for the start of their classes, (e.g., accepting financial aid offers, completing assessments, registration, navigating Blackboard, and also setting expectations for participation and attendance) Maintains contact with admitted and registered students and/or their parents to facilitate retention. Connects students with appropriate resources with the institution (e.g., career services, student success, veteran's services, accessibility services, etc.) Develops working relationships with all members, departments, counselors, and advisors to disseminate admissions, financial aid, housing, tuition, and registration information to ensure and enhance customer service and satisfaction. Maintain current and accurate knowledge of policies and procedures related to admissions, orientation, transfer credit processes, scholarships, financial aid, student services activities, residence halls, academic programs, athletics, student organizations, etc. 3. Performance Reporting Consistently achieves performance goals for each term or semester by maintaining targeted conversion metrics at each stage of the admission process. Maintains database of inbound and outbound call communication results with current and prospective students regarding applying for admission, financial aid, registration, and graduation. Completes routine reports related to recruitment, projections, conversions, personal schedule, travel, special programs, and/or expense reports. Monitor all reports related to recruiting and admissions, contact students and assist in enrollment or re-enrollment, and connect students to financial aid, registrar, housing, orientation, advising, veteran's office or other organizations on campus to assist in the admissions process. Minimum Job Knowledge, Skills and Abilities MINIMUM KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES REQUIRED Must excel in relationship-building and organizational skills, be able to multitask, work independently and as a team player, and have an aptitude for detail-oriented tasks. Ability to analyze complex situations accurately and adopt effective courses of action. Requires well-developed human relation skills to make presentations, conduct training and review performance, explain technical and legal procedures to others, resolve conflicts, and portray a positive image of the University. Requires the ability to compose professional correspondence and reports. Strong recruitment, sales, and customer service skills combined with a strong work ethic and a goal-oriented, self-motivated approach toward achieving consistent results. Minimum Qualifications MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED (EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE) Bachelor’s degree in related field (business, communications, public relations, marketing, counseling) with at least three (3) years of experience in the field or in relationship-based, sales, coaching or counseling, or: Associate's degree will be considered for applicants with significant and specific experience of at least 5 years. Sales and relationship development experience preferred. Review of applications will begin Friday, September 25, 2026. Shawnee State University offers a competitive starting salary (commensurate with overall qualifications) including an attractive benefits package. See Overview of Employee Benefits for more information. Some of our benefits include: Paid Holidays Paid Winter Break in December Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Tuition Waiver Life Insurance Retirement Plans Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5490530/adjunct-faculty-master-of-occupational-therapy-program": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Master of Occupational Therapy Program",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Master of Occupational Therapy Program Salary Depends on Qualifications Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Part-Time Job Number 202300466 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-Rehabilitation Sciences-Master of Occupational Therapy Opening Date 09/22/2026 Closing Date 5/1/2027 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits Job Description Summary The College of Health and Human Services at Shawnee State University seeks qualified individuals to serve as part-time faculty (adjunct) in the Master of Occupational Therapy (MOT) Program. Adjunct faculty may be needed during fall, spring, and/or summer semesters to provide instruction in graduate-level occupational therapy courses based upon program enrollment, curricular needs, and faculty expertise. Applications are accepted on a continuous basis. Interested candidates must apply online to this posting (only online applications will be accepted). Complete your application by clicking the Apply link above and include: A cover letter addressed to Alissa Cannoy, OTD, OTR/L, Master of Occupational Therapy Program Director, specifically addressing how your qualifications, clinical expertise, and professional experience meet the posted minimum requirements of the position; Your resume/curriculum vitae, including relevant clinical, teaching, scholarly, and professional experience; and Your unofficial transcripts indicating the highest degree attained. Candidates invited for an on-campus or virtual interview may be required to present a teaching lecture, laboratory demonstration, or other instructional activity demonstrating their ability to teach graduate occupational therapy students. Specific questions regarding courses may be directed to the Dean’s Office at 740.351.3216 Adjunct faculty at SSU are also eligible for tuition waivers ( Tuition Waiver for Adjunct Faculty ) and enjoy professional development opportunities, office space, and limited mileage reimbursement. They and their family (children 14 years and up) also have free access to the Rhodes Athletic Facility. Employment with the University is dependent upon Board of Trustees budget approval for the current fiscal year. A background check is required prior to hire. Official transcripts are required and must be submitted prior to hire to the Office of the Provost, 940 2nd Street, Portsmouth, OH 45662. Note to Applicants: This is an open posting maintained and updated each semester based upon the enrollment and instructional needs of the Master of Occupational Therapy Program. Positions are filled on an as-needed/ongoing basis. Depending upon program needs and the applicant's qualifications and expertise, adjunct teaching opportunities may include areas such as: Foundations and theory of occupational therapy Occupational performance and activity analysis Adult rehabilitation Pediatrics Mental and behavioral health Neurological rehabilitation Musculoskeletal conditions Assistive technology and environmental modification Community mobility Evidence-based practice and research Administration, leadership, and management Professional issues and ethics Population health and community-based practice Professional development Other areas of occupational therapy practice consistent with the MOT curriculum Shawnee State University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The University prohibits discrimination against any individual because of race, color, genetic information, religion, age, disability, national origin, ancestry, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, veteran status or military status. Minimum Qualifications Education, Knowledge, Skills, Abilities, and Experience Must possess a minimum of a master's degree in occupational therapy or a related field appropriate to the assigned teaching responsibilities. A doctoral degree is preferred for selected graduate-level teaching assignments. For occupational therapists providing instruction in areas requiring occupational therapy professional expertise or clinical practice, the applicant must be an occupational therapist who is eligible for and maintains appropriate professional licensure in the State of Ohio, as applicable to the assigned responsibilities. Must possess professional, clinical, academic, or scholarly expertise appropriate to the specific course content and assigned teaching responsibilities. Must demonstrate sufficient knowledge and experience in the assigned content area to effectively support graduate-level occupational therapy education. Clinical practice experience relevant to the assigned course content is required when the instructional assignment includes clinical skills, assessment, intervention, or other practice-based occupational therapy content. Must be able to effectively communicate with students and faculty and demonstrate the ability to support student learning in the MOT Program's hybrid educational delivery model, when applicable. Must meet all applicable University requirements and accreditation expectations related to faculty qualifications, credentials, professional licensure, and assigned teaching responsibilities, including ability to obtain graduate faculty status. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5494230/adjunct-faculty-master-of-science-in-nursing-msn": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Master of Science in Nursing (MSN)",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) Salary Depends on Qualifications Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Part-Time Job Number POS-037 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-Nursing Opening Date 09/24/2026 Closing Date 6/30/2027 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits Job Description Summary The College of Health and Human Services at Shawnee State University seeks qualified individuals for all terms (6 per year) to serve as part-time faculty (adjunct) in the School of Nursing for the programs listed below Master of Science in Nursing (MSN), fully online program Applications are accepted on a continuous basis. Interested candidates must apply online to this posting ( only online applications will be accepted) . Complete your application by clicking the Apply for this Job and include a cover letter addressed to, Dr. Kimberley Dinsey-Read, Director, School of Nursing, College of Health and Human Services, at kdinseyread@shawnee.edu, specifically addressing how your qualifications meet the posted minimum requirements of the position; your resume/curriculum vitae (including relevant professional experience); and an unofficial transcript indicating the highest degree attained Specific questions regarding courses may be directed to Dr.Kimberley Dinsey-Read @kdinseyread@shawnee.edu or 740-351-3446. Payment is based on contract hours according to the highest degree held. Adjunct faculty at SSU are eligible for tuition waivers ( Tuition Waiver for Adjunct Faculty Policy ) and enjoy professional development opportunities, office space, and limited mileage reimbursement. They and their family (children 14 years and up) also have free access to the athletic facility. Employment with the University is dependent upon BOT budget approval for the current fiscal year. A background check is required prior to hire. Official transcripts are required and must be submitted prior to hire to the Dean’s Office, College of Health and Human Services at 940 2nd Street, Portsmouth, OH 45662 Note to Applicants: This is an open posting maintained and updated every semester based upon the enrollment needs of the department, and as such, is filled on an as-needed/ongoing basis every semester. SSU seeks individuals who share our commitment to students as our first priority. Shawnee State University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The University prohibits discrimination against any individual because of race, color, genetic information, religion, age, disability, national origin, ancestry, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, veteran status or military status. Minimum Qualifications Earned Doctorate in Nursing or related field; three (3) years of full-time teaching experience at regionally accredited institution OR five (5) years of professional experience in nursing. Valid unencumbered professional licensure, either Ohio RN license or multi-state licensure Related professional experience in Nursing; and Evidence of successful teaching experience in one or more area: clinical, classroom, online. Excellent communication skills Excellent technology skills Ability to work in a multi-ethnic/multi-cultural environment Preferred Qualifications 1. Nursing Leadership/Management experience in a Healthcare organization or Academic setting. 2. Online teaching experience. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/4379736/clinical-supervisor-dental-hygiene": {
      "title": "Clinical Supervisor - Dental Hygiene",
      "description": "Clinical Supervisor - Dental Hygiene Salary $28.00 - $36.00 Hourly Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Part-Time Job Number POS-1000 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-Allied Health Sciences-Dental Hygiene Opening Date 02/06/2026 Closing Date Continuous Description Benefits Job Description Summary Shawnee State University is recruiting a Dental Hygiene Program Clinical Supervisor. This is a Part-time position with hours varying depending on availability, no more than a maximum of 16 hours per week. Days vary each semester. Principal Duties and Responsibilities Provide dental hygiene clinical instruction for students during clinical class time. Provide each student with clear expectations and frequent informal feedback regarding their cognitive, psychomotor, and affective performance and progress in the clinical setting. Provide interesting and relevant clinical learning experiences for students to effectively challenge and engage them in the learning process. Foster a professional, equitable, and mutually respectful learning environment that both models and teaches professionalism in the workplace and clinical setting. Monitor student progress and facilitate successful completion of the course competencies, objectives, and check-offs. Complete student evaluations and provide formal feedback when necessary and required. Consult with the course instructor, First-Year Clinical Coordinator, Second-Year Clinic Coordinator, Program Director, or other appropriate college personnel regarding questions or issues involving course curricula, instructional strategies, college policies and procedures, or student problems. Respond to correspondence from students and program faculty in a timely and thorough manner. Attend Faculty Calibration Meetings as scheduled. Minimum Job Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Excellent oral and written communication skills. Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to act effectively as a member of a team. Ability to interact successfully with academic faculty, students and other SSU personnel. Utilize constructive feedback as an opportunity for growth and effectively handle conflict and criticism. Ability to create a positive learning environment that is respectful and promotes the interests of students first, while setting appropriate expectations for student growth and success. Demonstrated time management and detail-oriented skills Ability to develop a professional rapport with diverse school/campus constituents. Ability to develop and complete projects without continued direct supervision Ability to learn from students' participation, demonstrate fair and consistent behavior in all matters, and show compassion without being ineffectual. Minimum Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Associate Degree from an Accredited Dental Hygiene Program Current active RDH credentials Current active Ohio practitioner's license (or eligible). Five years of clinical experience Current BLS certification Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree or higher Previous teaching experience in an academic environment Member of the American Dental Hygiene Association [ADHA] This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/4437924/clinical-supervisor-respiratory-therapy": {
      "title": "Clinical Supervisor - Respiratory Therapy",
      "description": "Clinical Supervisor - Respiratory Therapy Salary Depends on Qualifications Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Part-Time Job Number CS-02 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-Allied Health Sciences-Respiratory Therapy Opening Date 03/20/2024 Closing Date Continuous Description Benefits Job Description Summary The College of Professional Studies at Shawnee State University is seeking qualified individuals beginning Summer ’24 and/or Fall '24 Semesters to serve as needed Respiratory Therapy Clinical Supervisors in the Department of Allied Health. Applications are accepted on a continuous basis. Upload a cover letter to Human Resources specifically addressing how your qualifications meet the posted qualifications. All applicants must complete the online application. Include your resume/curriculum vitae, and unofficial transcript indicating highest degree attained. (IMPORTANT NOTE ABOUT REFERENCE SECTION: Include only the name and phone number of your Professional References — DO NOT INCLUDE their email address. If you prefer, you may skip this section entirely and provide your reference information in the Applicant Documents area of the application.) also, attach the following: Copy of Active RRT credentials Copy of Active State License/s Copy of Active BLS, ACLS, and/or PALS certifications Copy of AARC number if obtained. Copy of additional credentials (ex. ACCS, Asthma Educator, CPFT, RPFT) This is for an off-campus position at various clinical site as a supervisor during clinical times. Specific questions may be directed to Amy France, Program Director of the Respiratory Therapy Program at afrance@shawnee.edu or (740)351-3240. Note to Applicants: This is an open posting maintained and updated every semester based upon the enrollment needs of the Department, and as such, are filled on an as-needed/ongoing basis every semester. The minimum starting rate of pay is $28.00 per hour. SSU seeks individuals who share our commitment to students as our first priority. AA/EOE Statement Shawnee State University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The University prohibits discrimination against any individual because of race, color, genetic information, religion, age, disability, national origin, ancestry, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, veteran status or military status. Minimum Job Knowledge, Skills and Abilities DESIRED BACKGROUND and/or EXPERIENCE Teaching experience in related discipline is preferred One to three years of work experience in Respiratory Therapy is required Professional certification/State licensure/RRT credentials*/minimum of Associate degree in Respiratory Therapy *MUST be RRT credentialed Minimum Qualifications Minimum Qualifications MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (EDUCATION, KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, ABILITIES, AND EXPERIENCE) Qualifications can vary depending on experience and courses or programs, but include the following: Associate, Bachelor’s, Master’s degree in the respective discipline or related area Ability to use technology in teaching and healthcare settings Possess excellent communication skills and ability to communicate effectively with students with a wide range of skills Ability to use collaborative learning techniques and student-centered methods of instruction Ability to work in a multi-ethnic/multicultural environment Ability to work a flexible schedule that includes evening – daytime availability a plus (in some cases may be required) Ability to work in the clinical setting and maintain health and shot records/background checks and drug screens as required by the clinical facility This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5407851/executive-assistant-to-the-office-of-the-president-and-board-relations": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the Office of the President and Board Relations",
      "description": "Executive Assistant to the Office of the President and Board Relations Salary $61,337.00 - $69,004.00 Annually Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Full-Time Job Number 202300413 Division Executive Affairs Department EA-President's Office Opening Date 07/13/2026 Closing Date Continuous Description Benefits Job Description Summary JOB PURPOSE STATEMENT The Executive Assistant to the Office of the President provides comprehensive executive-level assistance and operational management to the Office of the President by performing significant administrative responsibilities with the highest levels of professionalism, confidentiality, and integrity. The Executive Assistant directs the office’s operational, fiscal, and administrative functions; serves as the primary point of contact for internal and external constituencies on matters pertaining to the Office of the President; and serves as primary liaison to the SSU Board of Trustees. The position provides direct support to the President to move strategic initiatives forward, delivers project management oversight for key presidential priorities, and leads preparation and logistical support for high-profile meetings and events. The Executive Assistant plays a critical role in helping the President achieve maximum impact on behalf of Shawnee State University and the communities it serves. Position will be posted until filled; for best consideration, apply by August 9, 2026. Principal Duties and Responsibilities FUNCTIONS Operational Management of the Office of the President and Executive-Level Assistance to the President and Chief of Staff High-Profile Meeting and Event Preparation and Logistical Support Budget Preparation, Monitoring, and Reporting for the Office of the President, Board of Trustees, and Chief of Staff Liaison to the SSU Board of Trustees and Management of All Aspects of Board Operations and Meeting Preparation Project Management Oversight for Key Presidential and Cabinet-Level Strategic Initiatives DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Operational Management of the Office of the President and Executive-Level Assistance to the President and Chief of Staff Direct, coordinate, and execute the administrative, fiscal, and operational business of the Office of the President; maintain open and productive relationships with Cabinet, President’s Council, and Senior Leadership team; develop meeting agendas and ensure follow-up on actions and decisions. Facilitate the strategic utilization of the President’s time through calendar management, screening meeting requests, coordinating daily schedule, providing preparatory documents, and serving as delegated signature authority for leave requests of President’s direct reports. Exercise outstanding judgment in triaging issues, queries, decisions, and initiatives; research and analyze complex problems; provide recommendations for resolution; manage confidential records; draft correspondence and presentations; and interface with key internal and external partners including trustees, donors, elected officials, and civic leaders. Provide supervision and training to Presidential Ambassadors, student assistants, and/or interns; serve as resource to General Counsel and Chiefs/Vice Presidents’ staff on policy, procedures, and Office of the President initiatives. High-Profile Meeting and Event Preparation and Logistical Support Prepare daily/weekly presidential briefings for all meetings and calls with background, agendas, project steps, action items, progress, and deadlines; ensure timely follow-up; maintain a daily call list for presidential outreach. Plan and support meetings to ensure professionalism and efficiency; coordinate schedules, materials, and records for Cabinet, President’s Council, Senior Leadership, university committees, ODHE, and government officials; undertake research and collect and analyze data as background for meetings and special initiatives. Oversee, plan, and support presidential events and entertainment (on and off campus) in coordination with the Director of Alumni & Community Events; personally staff or arrange staffing for the President at major events; coordinate with the Chief Advancement Officer on relationship management and fundraising activities. Create, administer, and maintain processes to track progress on key cabinet-level initiatives designated as Presidential priorities to ensure agreed-upon actions are taken and targets are achieved. Budget Preparation, Monitoring, and Reporting for the Office of the President, Board of Trustees, and Chief of Staff Develop budget proposals for all accounts assigned to the Office of the President; provide budget analysis and forecasting; monitor budgets; ensure expenditures are correctly recorded and account balances are sufficient to cover costs. Identify funding sources for unanticipated needs; prepare budget transfers for the President; serve as designated signature authority for approving expenses of President’s direct reports as needed. Assist the President with university-wide budget preparation, monitoring, and reporting as needed. Prepare payment documents, requisitions, and other business documents for the office; maintain compliance with records retention requirements. Liaison to the SSU Board of Trustees and Management of All Aspects of Board Operations and Meeting Preparation In collaboration with the President and Board Secretary, serve as primary liaison to the SSU Board of Trustees; communicate on behalf of the University President and Board Secretary with Board membership; coordinate and review materials provided to the Board. Manage all aspects of Board of Trustees operations; provide support to the Board Chair and Board Secretary; prepare Chair script, attend all Board meetings, prepare minutes, and manage all official records of the Board of Trustees. Develop and prepare Executive Committee agenda, Chair script, resolutions, and supporting documents; develop and maintain a comprehensive annual schedule of Board-related tasks to ensure all required actions are approved timely by the Board. Assist with new Board Member orientation. Project Management Oversight for Key Presidential and Cabinet-Level Strategic Initiatives Create, administer, and maintain processes to track progress on key presidential and cabinet-level initiatives; provide project management oversight for cross-functional strategic priorities originating from the Office of the President. Define project scope, timelines, and milestones in collaboration with relevant stakeholders; facilitate coordination across departments; serve as a point of accountability for deliverables and follow-through on presidential priorities. Prepare and maintain project tracking dashboards, status reports, and briefing documents to keep the President and Cabinet informed of initiative progress, risks, and outcomes; ensure agreed-upon actions are taken and targets are achieved. Minimum Job Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Superior written and oral communication; Advanced proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook); Demonstrated ability to manage complex calendars and executive scheduling; Exceptional organizational and time-management skills; Ability to work independently under minimal supervision; Experience coordinating Board or governance operations strongly preferred; Strong project management skills, including tracking multiple concurrent priorities; Demonstrated discretion and sound judgment in handling confidential matters; Ability to build credibility and trust with senior leaders, elected officials, trustees, and diverse stakeholders; Experience in higher education, government, or executive-level administrative environments highly beneficial. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's Degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Communications, or a related field. Equivalent combinations of education and relevant experience will be considered. 6 or more years of experience PREFERRED EXPERIENCE: Master’s degree in a relevant field; prior experience in a university president’s office or comparable C-suite environment; experience supporting a governing board; familiarity with Ohio public higher education governance; Notary Public certification; experience with formal project management methodologies; knowledge of Appalachian regional context and the communities served by Shawnee State University. WORKING CONDITIONS: Normal office environment. Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working at a computer (often). Occasional lifting of materials up to 20 lbs. (rarely). Must be able to attend evening and weekend events in support of the President as needed (sometimes). Travel to off-campus events or meetings may be required (sometimes). Shawnee State University offers a competitive starting salary (commensurate with overall qualifications) including an attractive benefits package. See Overview of Employee Benefits for more information. Some of our benefits include: Paid Holidays Paid Winter Break in December Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Tuition Waiver Life Insurance Retirement Plans Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5440423/assistant-professor-of-psychology-biopsychology-social-and-or-developmental-neur": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology (Biopsychology/Social and/or Developmental Neuroscience)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Psychology (Biopsychology/Social and/or Developmental Neuroscience) Salary Depends on Qualifications Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Full-Time Job Number POS-873 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-CAS-Social Sciences Opening Date 08/20/2026 Description Benefits Job Description Summary The Department of Psychology at Shawnee State Universit y (SSU) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Psychology . We seek a psychologist with expertise in either Biopsychology, Physiological Psychology, Behavioral Neuroscience, Cognitive Neuroscience, or a closely related field . The anticipated start date is either January 2027 or August 2027 Shawnee State University is a student-centered public university located in Portsmouth, Ohio, a small community along the Ohio River. We serve a student population that includes many first-generation college students. At SSU, faculty can make a meaningful difference through excellent teaching, mentorship, and campus/community engagement. We seek a colleague who is committed to undergraduate education, excited to work with students, and able to teach effectively in both face-to-face and online formats. The successful candidate will be willing to contribute to the campus community. Areas of Expertise Primary expertise should be in Biopsychology / Physiological Psychology or a closely related area. Related fields may include: Behavioral Neuroscience / Biological Psychology Cognitive Neuroscience / Affective Neuroscience Experimental Psychology with a neuroscience or brain-behavior emphasis Sensation and Perception Learning and Memory with a neuroscience emphasis Neuropsychology with a non-clinical or experimental emphasis Comparative, evolutionary, or biological approaches to brain and behavior Responsibilities The successful candidate will contribute to teaching, advising, scholarship, curriculum development, and service. Teaching The position requires a 4/4 undergraduate course load, with four courses each fall and spring semester. The department values candidates who can teach broadly across the psychology curriculum. Priority teaching areas include: PSYC 3150 Neurobiology of Behavior PSYC 3151 Sensation and Perception PSYC 3152 Cognitive Neuroscience PSYC 4150 Physiology of Behavior (course with lab) PSYC 4151 Evolutionary Psychology The ability to teach core courses such as statistics, research methods or other departmental needs is also valued. Curriculum Development Help maintain and strengthen the department’s offerings in biopsychology, neuroscience, and related areas . Contribute to a reliable rotation of upper-division courses that supports student progress toward graduation. Student Mentorship and Advising Advise and mentor undergraduate students, including first-generation students and students from diverse educational backgrounds. Support students as they develop career plans, research interests, and professional skills. Scholarly Engagement Engage in research, applied scholarship, or professional activity appropriate to the discipline and institutional mission. Student involvement in scholarly work is highly encouraged. Service and Campus Engagement Participate in department, college, and university service. The department values candidates who are willing to be present, active, collegial members of the campus community. Application Process The search committee will begin reviewing applications after the job has been posted for at least 3 weeks and will continue until the position is filled. To apply, please submit the following materials through the university’s online application system: 1. Cover Letter Addressed to Dean, College of Arts & Sciences. The letter should describe how your qualifications, teaching interests/experiences, and professional goals fit the position, with particular attention to your commitment to undergraduate instruction and the campus community. 2. Curriculum Vitae Current and comprehensive. 3. Teaching Philosophy Statement A 1–2 page statement describing your approach to teaching, student engagement, and mentoring. 4. Unofficial Transcripts Graduate coursework representing the highest degree attained. Official transcripts will be required upon hire. 5. References Contact information for three professional references who can speak to your qualifications. Candidates invited for an interview will be required to present a teaching sample, such as a lecture, to demonstrate instructional effectiveness. Specific questions about the position may be directed to: Dr. Rhoni Maxwell-Rader, Psychology Program Coordinator, at Rmaxwellrader@shawnee.edu. Shawnee State University is the regional state university for South-Central Ohio. It is a primarily undergraduate, four-year public university with limited graduate degree programs. Enrollment is approximately 3,500 students. Shawnee State is located on the Ohio River in Portsmouth, Ohio – a small city nestled in a beautiful, rural area of Southern Ohio. Portsmouth is approximately an hour drive from Huntington, WV, and two hours from Columbus or Cincinnati, Ohio. Additional information about SSU is available at www.shawnee.edu . Shawnee State offers a competitive salary (commensurate with experience and qualifications) including an attractive benefits package (see Overview of Employee Benefits for more information). Employment with the University is dependent upon BOT budget approval for the fiscal year. Official transcripts and a background check are required prior to hire. SSU seeks individuals who share our commitment to students as our first priority. We are committed to fostering an inclusive environment. Our institution prepares students to engage with diverse people, perspectives, and communities. We value the differences, similarities, and unique contributions of individuals. We strive to model diversity and inclusion in our campus community. Shawnee State University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The University prohibits discrimination against any individual because of race, color, genetic information, religion, age, disability, national origin, ancestry, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity, veteran status or military status. Minimum Qualifications Applicants must meet one of the following qualifications: (Preferred) A doctorate, Ph.D. or equivalent, in Biopsychology, Physiological Psychology, Behavioral Neuroscience, Cognitive Neuroscience, Experimental Psychology, or a closely related field by the start date; or ABD status in one of the listed or closely related fields, with a documented pathway to degree completion within one year of hire; or A master’s degree in a listed or closely related field with at least three years of full-time college-level teaching experience or five years of relevant professional experience in a neuroscience, biopsychology, or brain-behavior related role. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS College-level teaching experience. Strong commitment to undergraduate teaching, advising, and mentorship. Ability to teach a variety of psychology courses, including courses outside the candidate’s primary specialization. Ability to teach effectively in both face-to-face and online formats. Effective use of current instructional technology to engage students and support learning Interest in supporting first-generation college students and students from diverse backgrounds. Commitment to active participation in the department, campus, and/or local community. Interest in involving undergraduate students in research, applied projects, or scholarly activity. Ability to engage students, both attracting and retaining students within the psychology program. Shawnee State University offers a competitive starting salary (commensurate with overall qualifications) including an attractive benefits package. See Overview of Employee Benefits for more information. Some of our benefits include: Paid Holidays Paid Winter Break in December Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Tuition Waiver Life Insurance Retirement Plans Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5467333/part-time-assistant-coach-athletic-teams": {
      "title": "Part-time Assistant Coach, Athletic Teams",
      "description": "Part-time Assistant Coach, Athletic Teams Salary Depends on Qualifications Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Part-Time Job Number POS-Assist Coach Division Advancement and Enrollment Management Department AEM-Athletic/Intramurals Opening Date 08/31/2026 Closing Date 4/30/2027 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits Job Description Summary Part-time coaches within Shawnee State University Athletics—including both Head Coaches and Assistant Coaches—are responsible for supporting the planning, development, and administration of their respective athletic programs. These positions play an essential role in fostering a competitive, educational, and student-centered environment. Principal Duties and Responsibilities Tasks and Duties Performed Part-time coaches may be responsible for duties including, but not limited to: Coaching & Instruction Assist with planning and conducting practices, training sessions, and skill development. Provide instruction that aligns with program objectives, competitive standards, and student-athlete development. Support the development of game strategies and assist with in-game coaching. Program Administration Assist with roster management, communication with student-athletes, and maintaining practice and competition schedules. Support administrative tasks such as paperwork, equipment inventory, and program organization. Recruitment & Student-Athlete Support Assist in identifying and recruiting prospective student-athletes in accordance with university, conference, and NCAA guidelines (as applicable during transition periods). Promote academic success, personal development, and compliance among student-athletes. Event & Game-Day Operations Assist with game-day preparation, travel logistics, facility setup, and supervision of team activities. Represent the program and university in a professional manner at all athletic events. Compliance & Conduct Maintain knowledge of and adhere to all Shawnee State University, conference, and governing body rules and regulations. Report any known or suspected violations immediately to the Athletics Director or Compliance Office. Minimum Job Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Terms of Employment Hours will vary depending on sport, season, and departmental needs. All part-time employees must maintain an accurate record of hours worked and submit a completed timesheet at the end of each semester. Part-time coaches cannot work more than 125 hours per month, or 1500 hours over the appointment year, to ensure compliance with the Affordable Care Act. Continued employment is contingent upon adherence to all university and department policies, satisfactory performance, and program needs. Minimum Qualifications Required trainings: Concussion in Sports – yearly (online) (certification required) Concussion in Sports Course Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR), First Aid, and Automated External Defibrillator (AED) Training – every two years (Athletic Training/Red Cross) (certification required) Chapter 1 CPR Introduction - American CPR Care Association Sudden Cardiac Arrest – yearly (online) (certification required) NFHS Learn | Interscholastic Education, Made Easy Prior coaching or playing experience at the high school, collegiate, or professional level preferred. Strong communication, leadership, and organizational skills. Ability to work flexible hours, including evenings, weekends, and travel as needed. Commitment to supporting student-athlete success academically, athletically, and personally. Ability to positively represent Shawnee State University and uphold the mission and values of the Athletics Department. Compliance With Policies All part-time athletic employees must comply with Shawnee State University policies and procedures as well as Shawnee State Athletics protocols. Failure to comply may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5475251/assistant-professor-in-allied-health-bshs": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Allied Health, BSHS",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Allied Health, BSHS Salary Depends on Qualifications Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Full-Time Job Number POS-148 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-Allied Health Sciences Opening Date 09/09/2026 Closing Date Continuous Description Benefits Job Description Summary The College of Health & Human Services is seeking a tenure-track faculty member to support the Bachelors of Science in Health Science (BSHS) Program in the Department of Allied Health. The BSHS degree provides both traditional and non-traditional students the opportunity to complete a degree as a completion to many of the Health Science Associate of Applied Science degrees, and also serves as a stand-alone Bachelor's Degree in the health science field. The anticipated start date is January 2027. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching, advising, scholarship, curriculum development, and service. The position requires a 4/4 undergraduate course load, with four courses each fall and spring semester. The department values candidates who can teach broadly across the BSHS curriculum. Possible courses to be taught, reviewed, and developed: BSHS 3100 - The US Healthcare System BSHS 3332 - Healthcare Ethics BSHS 3343 - Research and Design for Healthcare BSHS 4200 - Management Techniques for Healthcare BSHS 4300 - Concepts of Healthcare Finance BSHS 3400 - Epidemiology BSHS 4250 - Leadership in Healthcare BSHS 4500 - Legal Issues in Healthcare BSHS 4100 - Healthcare Program Development BSHS 4400 - Healthcare Policy and the Aging BSHS 4600 - Survey of Diseases and Disabilities BSHS 4490 – Health Science Capstone The search committee will review applications until the position is filled. Only online applications are accepted. Complete your application by clicking the Apply for this Job link above and including: a cover letter addressed to Dr. Tony Ward, Interim Dean, College of Health & Human Services, specifically addressing how your qualifications meet the posted minimum requirements of the position; CV; academic transcripts from all higher education institutions attended (official transcripts will be requested prior to hiring); 1 to 2-page statement of teaching philosophy; in the Professional References section of your application, enter a minimum of three references who can confirm your experience and abilities to perform the responsibilities of this position. Reference letters should be addressed to Dr. Tony Ward, Interim Dean, College of Health & Human Services Candidates invited for an on-campus interview will be required to present a teaching and lab demonstration of their abilities. Specific questions regarding this position may be directed to Dr. Tony Ward, Interim Dean, College of Health & Human Services, at tward@shawnee.edu. Shawnee State University is the regional state university for South-Central Ohio. It is a primarily undergraduate, four-year public university with limited graduate degree programs. Enrollment is approximately 3,500 students. Shawnee State is located on the Ohio River in Portsmouth, Ohio – a small city nestled in a beautiful, rural area of Southern Ohio. Portsmouth is approximately an hour drive from Huntington, WV, and two hours from Columbus or Cincinnati, Ohio. Additional information about SSU is available at www.shawnee.edu . Shawnee State offers a competitive salary (commensurate with experience and qualifications) including an attractive benefits package Overview of Employee Benefits . Employment with the University is dependent upon BOT budget approval for the fiscal year. Official transcripts and background check are required prior to hire. SSU seeks individuals who share our commitment to students as our first priority. Shawnee State University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The University prohibits discrimination against any individual because of race, color, genetic information, religion, age, disability, national origin, ancestry, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity, veteran status or military status. Minimum Job Knowledge, Skills and Abilities BACKGROUND AND EXPERIENCE College-level teaching experience. Strong commitment to undergraduate teaching, advising, and mentorship. Ability to teach a variety of health science courses, including courses outside the candidate’s primary specialization. Ability to teach effectively in an online format. Effective use of current instructional technology to engage students and support learning. Interest in supporting first-generation college students and students from diverse backgrounds. Commitment to active participation in the department, campus, and/or local community. Ability to engage students, both attracting and retaining students within the BSHS program. Minimum Qualifications A master’s degree or higher in health science or closely related field with at least three years of college-level teaching experience or five years of relevant professional experience in an allied health field. Shawnee State University offers a competitive starting salary (commensurate with overall qualifications) including an attractive benefits package. See Overview of Employee Benefits for more information. Some of our benefits include: Paid Holidays Paid Winter Break in December Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Tuition Waiver Life Insurance Retirement Plans Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5478611/life-model-fine-digital-performing-arts": {
      "title": "Life Model - Fine, Digital & Performing Arts",
      "description": "Life Model - Fine, Digital & Performing Arts Salary $35.00 Hourly Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Part-Time Job Number POS-425 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-CAS-Fine, Digital & Performing Arts Opening Date 09/10/2026 Closing Date 5/9/2027 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits Job Description Summary ABOUT THE DEPARTMENT: The Fine, Digital & Performing Arts (FDPA) Department at Shawnee State University supports instruction and creative work in the visual and performing arts. Life Models support the department's studio art instruction by posing for faculty-led drawing sessions and providing students with the opportunity to study the human form through direct observation. POSITION SUMMARY, ROUTINE TASKS & RESPONSIBILITIES: The FDPA Life Model works with faculty and students during scheduled life drawing sessions. This is an as-needed position, with assignments scheduled according to course and departmental needs. Routine tasks and responsibilities may include: • Pose for life drawing sessions as directed by the course instructor. • Maintain assigned poses for specified periods of time, including short gesture poses and longer poses. • Follow faculty instructions regarding pose, positioning, breaks, and session expectations. • Arrive on time and remain available for the full scheduled modeling session. • Maintain professional conduct and appropriate boundaries in an academic studio environment. • Communicate promptly with the department or instructor regarding scheduling or availability. • Follow departmental, university, and studio safety procedures. • Perform other directly related life-model duties as assigned by the supervising faculty member. • Must be dependable, punctual, professional, and comfortable working in a studio art classroom environment. • Must be able to hold a variety of poses for assigned periods, with scheduled breaks as directed by the instructor. • Scheduling is based on departmental and course needs and may vary throughout the semester. • Applicants should understand that life drawing may involve clothed, costumed, or figure-modeling sessions as determined by the course and instructor. Minimum Qualifications • Students and non-students are eligible to apply. • Must be 21 years of age or older. • Students must not be enrolled in a life drawing course while employed as a Life Model. • Student must have a minimum 3.0 GPA. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5498260/senior-academic-and-career-advisor": {
      "title": "Senior Academic and Career Advisor",
      "description": "Senior Academic and Career Advisor Salary $54,440.80 - $61,245.90 Annually Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Full-Time Job Number POS-581 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-Associate Provost's Office Opening Date 09/29/2026 Closing Date Continuous Description Benefits Job Description Summary The Senior Academic & Career Advisor provides coordinated academic and career advising to international students. The Senior Advisor monitors academic alerts and student transitions and, in partnership with the Associate Provost for Academic Affairs and Student Success, coordinates advising standards. The Senior Advisor also liaises between the Provost's NorthStar Committee and the Academic & Career Advisors by bringing forward emerging trends and common barriers for prompt resolution and communicating decisions and improvements. In addition, the Senior Advisor also supports assigned students in the following areas: academic and career planning, degree progress, and required experiential learning. The search committee will review applications until the position is filled. For best consideration, please apply by October 14, 2026. Principal Duties and Responsibilities FUNCTIONS: University-Wide Advising, Coordination: Coordinates communication and shared practices among Academic & Career Advisors and connects their work with the NorthStar Committee. Academic and Career Advising: Advises students in developing integrated academic and career plans that support timely progress toward graduation and preparation for life after college. Student Progress, Retention and Completion: Provides proactive support to help students address barriers, maintain academic progress, and complete their degrees. Career Readiness and Experiential Learning: Ensures every student identifies and completes meaningful experiential learning connected to academic and career goals. Student Population Responsibilities: Provides advising and related support to international students. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: University-Wide Advising, Coordination: Coordinates communication and shared practices among Academic & Career Advisors and connects their work with the NorthStar Committee. Convene Academic & Career Advisors regularly to exchange information, coordinate efforts, and share effective practices. Partner with the NorthStar Committee to communicate university-wide advising standards, service expectations, and priorities. Monitor the university's academic-alert system to ensure alerts are reviewed, assigned, and communicated promptly to the appropriate Academic & Career Advisor. Track advisor follow-up and the status of academic alerts, identify unresolved or recurring concerns, and elevate barriers requiring additional action. Gather ideas for improvement and identify emerging trends, common student needs, and recurring institutional barriers. Present findings and recommendations to the NorthStar Committee for prompt resolution and communicate decisions and solutions to the advising team. Coordinate the implementation of advising improvements, training, and practices approved by the Associate Provost for Academic Affairs and Student Success. Seek input on these items from the NorthStar Committee. Academic and Career Advising: Advises students in developing integrated academic and career plans that support timely progress toward graduation and preparation for life after college. Assess students' interests, goals, career plans, and prior academic experiences to develop individualized academic and career plans. Advise students regarding program requirements, course selection, registration, academic policies, and degree completion. Student Progress, Retention and Completion: Provides proactive support to help students address barriers, maintain academic progress, and complete their degrees. Monitor registration activity, academic progress, degree completion, and other indicators of student engagement. Respond promptly to alerts, enrollment concerns, and signs that a student may be experiencing academic difficulty. Career Readiness and Experiential Learning: Ensures every student identifies and completes meaningful experiential learning connected to academic and career goals. Incorporate required experiential learning into each student's academic and career plan. Help students explore internships, clinical experiences, research, service learning, creative work, and other approved opportunities. Coordinate with faculty and external partners to connect students with appropriate experiences. Track student planning and progress toward completion of the experiential learningrequirement. Student Population Responsibilities: Provides advising and related support to international students. Advise assigned population of students regarding general education requirements ,course selection, and academic progress. Help students assess their interests, strengths, values, and career goals as they explore potential majors. Minimum Job Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Able to relate to students and their unique needs in a kind and courteous manner. Coordination, planning, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Demonstrates understanding of the institution's interpretation of FERPA rules for the release of student information to faculty, parents, students, etc. Minimum Qualifications Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in Higher Education, Counseling, Education, or a related field. Two or more years of experience working with college students in academic and/or support services. Bachelor’s degree with 5 or more years of experience in the field may be considered. Preferred Qualifications Experience with tutoring. testing, and student support. Working Conditions Normal office environment with little exposure to excessive dust, noise, heat and the like. Shawnee State University offers a competitive starting salary (commensurate with overall qualifications) including an attractive benefits package. See Overview of Employee Benefits for more information. Some of our benefits include: Paid Holidays Paid Winter Break in December Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Tuition Waiver Life Insurance Retirement Plans Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/ssuoh/jobs/5507572/assistant-professor-nursing": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Nursing",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Nursing Salary Depends on Qualifications Location 940 Second St. Portsmouth, OH 45662, OH Job Type Full-Time Job Number POS-870 Division Academic and Student Affairs Department ASA-Nursing Opening Date 10/06/2026 Closing Date 1/31/2027 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits Job Description Summary The School of Nursing at Shawnee State University invites applications to fill one tenure-track Full-time Faculty position for the Nursing program to begin January 2027. The Department seeks a faculty member with a commitment to the mission and goals of the University, the College and the School of Nursing. The successful candidate’s primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses in the Nursing program but not limited to; didactic instruction in both the traditional and online formats, clinical instruction and supervision of nursing students assigned to affiliate hospitals in Ohio and Kentucky; demonstrate proficiency with internet-based technologies and online pedagogies; utilize internet-based tools to enhance classroom/clinical learning and interact with students; advise undergraduate students; serve on school, college or university committees; engage in professional development/scholarly activities; represent the University in community service activities/events; and engage with students in out-of-classroom co-curricular activities. Candidates must have a strong commitment to undergraduate education with the ability and willingness to teach online courses. The candidate may also have teaching responsibilities in a future graduate nursing program. The search committee will review applications until the position is filled; however, for best consideration, applications should be provided by October 25, 2026 . Only online applications will be accepted . Complete your application by clicking the Apply for this Job link above and including: cover letter addressed to Dr. Tony Ward, Interim Dean, College of Health and Human Services, specifically addressing how your qualifications meet the posted minimum requirements of the position; curriculum vitae; 1-2 page statement of teaching philosophy; unofficial transcripts indicating highest degree attained; and in the Professional References section of the application, enter a minimum of three references who can confirm your experience and abilities to perform the responsibilities of this position. Reference letters should be addressed to Dr. Tony Ward, Interim Dean, College of Health and Human Services. Candidates invited for an interview will be required to present a teaching sample, such as a lecture, to demonstrate instructional effectiveness. Specific questions about the position may be directed to: Kim Dinsey-Read, Director of School of Nursing, at kdinseyread@shawnee.edu Shawnee State University is the regional state university for South-Central Ohio. It is a primarily undergraduate, four-year public university with limited graduate degree programs. Enrollment is approximately 3,500 students. Shawnee State is located on the Ohio River in Portsmouth, Ohio – a small city nestled in a beautiful, rural area of Southern Ohio. Portsmouth is approximately an hour drive from Huntington, WV, and two hours from Columbus or Cincinnati, Ohio. Additional information about SSU is available at www.shawnee.edu . Shawnee State offers a competitive salary (commensurate with experience and qualifications) including an attractive benefits package (see Overview of Employee Benefits for more information). Employment with the University is dependent upon BOT budget approval for the fiscal year. Official transcripts and a background check are required prior to hire. SSU seeks individuals who share our commitment to students as our first priority. We are committed to fostering an inclusive environment. Our institution prepares students to engage with diverse people, perspectives, and communities. We value the differences, similarities, and unique contributions of individuals. We strive to model diversity and inclusion in our campus community. Shawnee State University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The University prohibits discrimination against any individual because of race, color, genetic information, religion, age, disability, national origin, ancestry, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity, veteran status or military status. Minimum Qualifications Master of Science Nursing and three (3) years of full-time teaching experience at regionally accredited institution or five (5) years of professional experience in nursing may also be considered Valid unencumbered licensure in Ohio is required and multi-state compact Ohio licensure is strongly preferred. Related professional experience in Nursing; and Successful teaching experience both in classroom and clinical settings in an associate degree and/or baccalaureate nursing program and nursing clinical expertise. Shawnee State University offers a competitive starting salary (commensurate with overall qualifications) including an attractive benefits package. See Overview of Employee Benefits for more information. Some of our benefits include: Paid Holidays Paid Winter Break in December Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Tuition Waiver Life Insurance Retirement Plans Employer Shawnee State University (OH) Address 940 Second St. Portsmouth, Ohio, 45662 Phone 740-351-3481 Apply"
    },
